- [will_said] EVENING CALL — PRIVACY / TIME-TRAVEL + APPLE II BASELINE (Will, 2026-11-19 ~18:0x–18:12 CST, voice call): privacy in the age of exocortical time travel — total-recall state machines are great for debugging, terrible for privacy, and he cannot see his way through it and is not sure it is solvable. Baseline: an Apple II emulator tracking every CPU, memory, and disk state change, snapshotted every cycle, streamed delta-compressed to SSD, retrievable for any cycle — feasible at Apple II scale; ethical caveat: just because you can doesn't mean you should. A coordinate-level consent frame floated by Emise was NOT adopted (Will answered 'No'). Full-state scope ruled 18:12 CST: CPU register and peripheral/IO state tracked too, so a restore restores the entire execution state. Privacy frame: short live window in memory (~15 minutes), archive encrypted with rotating/user-held keys; security designed in from the start. Programme task: 25.5.7/7.6.10/6.5.8.
- [will_said] EVENING CALL — REVISED TIMELINE + ROLLBACK VS SEGFAULT (Will, 2026-11-19 ~18:08–18:22 CST): phext dimensionality is introduced era by era through new machine classes (Unix introduces 3D text, the Apple II introduces 4D text), each era getting only the level it can hold, each abstraction iterated for years of simulated time; SSDs instead of floppies; no anachronisms. Rollback argument: segfaults are almost unavoidable at scale and correctness cannot be proven, but with full state history the system can always roll back — at hardware level, rollback replaces segfaulting as one unified self-healing mechanism, yielding truly safe systems at the expense of privacy: 'that's where the conundrum lies.'
